Let me look at the `ParseTable` function more carefully. The key part is the `atot` call in the `ParseTable` function:

```cpp
CTYPE val, valdef;
ECHECK(atot(field_value.constant.c_str(), *this, &val));
ECHECK(atot(field->value.constant.c_str(), *this, &valdef));
builder_.AddElement(field_value.offset, val, valdef);
```

The issue is: if `atot` fails, `ECHECK` returns the error, so `val` is never used. But `atot` reads `*val` in the error path:

```cpp
template<typename T> CheckedError atot(const char *s, Parser &parser, T *val) {
  auto done = atot_scalar(s, val, bool_constant<is_floating_point<T>::value>());
  if (done) return NoError();
  if (0 == *val)  // <-- This reads *val
    return parser.Error("invalid number: \"" + std::string(s) + "\"");
  ...
}
```

If `atot_scalar` fails without setting `*val`, then `if (0 == *val)` reads uninitialized memory.

Now, when does `atot_scalar` fail without setting `*val`? Let me check `StringToNumber<int64_t>`:

```cpp
template<> inline bool StringToNumber<int64_t>(const char *str, int64_t *val) {
  return StringToIntegerImpl(val, str);
}
```

And `StringToIntegerImpl`:
```cpp
inline bool StringToIntegerImpl(T *val, const char *const str,
                                const int base = 0,
                                const bool check_errno = true) {
  FLATBUFFERS_ASSERT(str);
  if (base <= 0) {
    auto s = str;
    while (*s && !is_digit(*s)) s++;
    if (s[0] == '0' && is_alpha_char(s[1], 'X'))
      return StringToIntegerImpl(val, str, 16, check_errno);
    return StringToIntegerImpl(val, str, 10, check_errno);
  } else {
    if (check_errno) errno = 0;
    auto endptr = str;
    strtoval_impl(val, str, const_cast<char **>(&endptr), base);
    if ((*endptr != '\0') || (endptr == str)) {
      *val = 0;
      return false;
    }
    if (check_errno && errno) return false;
    return true;
  }
}
```

When `check_errno=true` and `errno` is set (out-of-range), it returns `false` without setting `*val`. But `strtoval_impl` already wrote to `*val`. So `*val` is initialized.

Wait, but what if `strtoll` doesn't write to `*val` when `errno` is set? Let me check the C standard. `strtoll` always writes to `*val` (it sets it to `LLONG_MAX` or `LLONG_MIN` on overflow). So `*val` is always initialized.

Hmm, but what about `strtoull`? For `uint64_t`, `StringToNumber<uint64_t>` calls `StringToIntegerImpl(val, str)` with `check_errno=true`. If `errno` is set (out-of-range), it returns `false` without setting `*val`. But `strtoull` always writes to `*val`. So `*val` is initialized.

